gcm/live-draw: write-backs of several surfaces share one readback submit and one GPU wait.

Symptom (Killzone 3, pass 20): patch 2017 writes every main-memory render target back at the first guest sync
point after it was drawn; KZ3 has four of them per frame (640x360, 320x180, 640x360, 320x180 F_X32) at one
semaphore release, plus the NV0039 scene copy. Each write-back created a committed readback buffer, recorded the
copy, submitted the command list and waited for the whole queue: four allocations and four full GPU waits per
release.

Fix: ld_writeback_batch records the copy of every surface into persistent readback buffers (16 slots, grown on
demand, rounded to 1 MB), submits once (ld_flush LD_FLUSH_READBACK), waits once, then converts all of them into
guest memory (RGBA8 -> A,R,G,B; FP16 and R32F byte-swapped). Both callers use it: the sync-point write-back
(rsx_live_draw_before_guest_sync) and the NV0039 range write-back (rsx_live_draw_writeback_range).
LD_WRITEBACK_BATCH=0 keeps the per-surface path for an A/B. rsx_live_draw_debug_writeback_stats reports totals
(time, calls, surfaces, GPU waits); [main-surface-writeback] prints them.

Verification: tools/smoke/test_wb_batch.c (KZ3-shaped frame, 120 frames under Proton, RTX 4060): release with
four main-memory surfaces 8.8-9.2 ms -> 0.63 ms, NV0039 copy segment 3.8-4.1 ms -> 2.2-2.4 ms; 9600/9600 checks
in both modes; LD_MAIN_SURFACE_WRITEBACK=0 fails (negative control).

